                <pb facs="0632" n="544" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv02/pg544"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">102.1/1518: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-12-19T13:00:00-05:00"
                            >December 19, 1918—1 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6674. For Crosby from Rathbone. Treasury 649. Embassy’s 6272, December 13, 12
                    p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">7 p.m.</hi>]. Your 682.<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d454-f1"/> First: Treasury not disposed to press France to discuss
                    at this time questions relating to whether and to what extent we will convert
                    our demand loans into long time obligations. Second: Secretary Glass agrees with
                    all the views set forth in Treasury 615,<anchor n="9" xml:id="d454-f2"/>
                    notwithstanding paragraphs second and fourth of your 682.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
